        <h1 class="cf-title">Watched vs. Consulted</h1>
        <p class="cf-subtitle">How the moon is rightly determined — and why convenience was never faithfulness.</p>
      </header>

      <h2>Three Methods, Only One Biblical</h2>

      <p>There are three ways people determine when a new month begins, and they produce different answers. Two of them come from men. One of them comes from Yahuah.</p>

      <ul>
        <li>The <strong>consulted</strong> method — mathematical tables predict when the moon <em>should</em> be new, and are then adjusted by rule so that certain dates never fall where the reckoning puts them. This is the Hillel II rabbinic system from 358 AD.</li>
        <li>The <strong>sighted</strong> method — watching for the first visible sliver of light in the western sky, and beginning the month when it is seen and reported.</li>
        <li>The <strong>renewal</strong> method — the month opens when the moon passes the greater light and turns back toward it, and the dawn that follows breaks. This is the biblical method.</li>
      </ul>

      <p>The three can disagree by a day or two each month — and over the course of a year, by a full month. Which one a believer chooses determines when they keep Yahuah's feasts. <strong>So this is not a small or academic question.</strong></p>

      <h2>What Chodesh Actually Says</h2>

      <p>The Hebrew word <strong><em>chodesh</em></strong> comes from <em>chadash</em> — to renew, to make new, to rebuild. <strong>It is a word about renewal, not about a man's eyesight.</strong> A thing is renewed at the moment it turns, not at the moment somebody happens to notice it has turned. The moon's turning is her passing of the greater light, after which her waning ends and her rebuilding begins. That is the renewal the word names.</p>

      <p>Hanok describes the same cycle in detail. On the first day a fourteenth part of her light appears when she rises; her light grows through fourteen days until it is full; and it wanes back to nothing (78:6, 78:11, 78:8). <strong>A small light on day one, growing.</strong> Not a dark month, and not a full one.</p>

      <blockquote class="cf-scripture">
        <em>"And Elohim said, Let there be lights in the firmament of the heaven… and let them be for signs, and for seasons, and for days, and years."</em>
        <span class="ref">— Genesis 1:14</span>
      </blockquote>

      <p>The moon is a <strong>light</strong> — hung in the heavens to give signs. And she is visible for most of every month, waxing and waning where anyone can watch her. <strong>The sign is her whole cycle, and the renewal is the pivot of it.</strong> The thin light that follows a night or two later is a real confirmation, and it is worth going out to see. It simply is not the thing that opened the month.</p>

      <h2>One Earth, One Day</h2>

      <p>Here is the difficulty with a sighted rule, and it is not a small one. <strong>Visibility is local.</strong> Whether the first thin light can be seen on a given evening depends on latitude, on the season, on the angle of the moon's path, and on the weather. Two believers on opposite sides of the earth, both faithful, both watching, will see it on different evenings — and will keep the same appointed time a day apart.</p>

      <p>A rule that hands two men two different days has not given them one appointed time. And a <em>moed</em> is a meeting. Yahuah did not appoint a meeting that begins on different days for different people.</p>

      <p>The moon's passing of the greater light is a single instant. It is the same instant for every man alive, whatever his sky looks like and whatever his clock says. <strong>It is the only reckoning under which the whole earth keeps one appointed time on one day</strong> — and it is why the second-day patch, added by others to cover the uncertainty of a sighting, has never been needed here.</p>

      <blockquote class="cf-scripture">
        <em>"The heavens declare the glory of Elohim; and the firmament sheweth his handywork. Day unto day uttereth speech, and night unto night sheweth knowledge."</em>
        <span class="ref">— Psalm 19:1–2</span>
      </blockquote>

      <p>The heavens <strong>declare</strong>. They <strong>show</strong>. They <strong>utter speech</strong>. This is the role Yahuah gave the lights — they are not silent witnesses to be worked around. <strong>They are active, speaking witnesses, and what they speak is an event in the sky, not a number on a page.</strong></p>

      <h2>Witnesses in the Sky, Not Tables on a Page</h2>

      <p>The distinction that matters is not whether a thing can be reckoned. It is whether the reckoning answers to the heavens or replaces them.</p>

      <p>Reckoning the moon's passing is reading a sign Yahuah hung in the sky. The event is real, it happens whether or not anyone is watching, and the reckoning simply names when it happened. <strong>Nothing about it is invented, and nothing about it can be moved.</strong></p>

      <p>The consulted calendar is a different act entirely. It does not merely predict the passing — it then <em>adjusts</em> the answer. Days are pushed forward and held back by rule so that certain dates never land where the reckoning puts them, because landing there is inconvenient. <strong>Once a day can be moved for convenience, it has stopped being an appointment and become a preference.</strong> That is the fault, and it has nothing to do with arithmetic.</p>

      <h2>Where the Consulted Calendar Came From</h2>

      <p>It is worth understanding <em>why</em> the consulted method exists at all. It was not always the rabbinic standard.</p>

      <p>For most of the history of Yahuah's people, the new moon was watched for and announced. Witnesses reported. Trumpets sounded. Beacons were lit on the hilltops to spread the word from Jerusalem outward. This worked while Yahuah's people lived in or near the land.</p>

      <p>After the Roman destruction of the temple in 70 AD, and especially after the dispersion that followed, the Jewish people were scattered across the empire. Coordinating a single day across vast distances became increasingly difficult. By 358 AD, the rabbi Hillel II abandoned the heavens altogether and published a calculated calendar that could be followed anywhere without reference to the sky — and then attached the postponements that let it be steered.</p>

      <p>The consulted calendar was a <em>practical compromise</em> — chosen because it was easier than trusting Yahuah to preserve His own calendar. <strong>It was never claimed to be more accurate. It was never claimed to be more biblical. It was claimed to be more convenient.</strong> Convenience is not faithfulness, and the Father did not ask His people to do what was convenient.</p>

      <h2>How It Works in Practice</h2>

      <p>Keeping the month is not difficult, but it does require attention to a few basics:</p>

      <ul>
        <li>The month renews when the moon passes the greater light. <strong>The dawn that follows opens day one.</strong></li>
        <li>The same instant belongs to the whole earth, so <strong>no report from anywhere else is needed</strong> and no two believers are ever a day apart.</li>
        <li>A night or two later the thin light comes out low in the west, sets quickly, and is worth going out to see. <strong>It confirms what already began.</strong></li>
        <li>On the fifteenth she stands full. If she does not, the count has slipped and can be corrected on the spot.</li>
        <li>The trumpet sounds at the beginning of the month, as commanded (Numbers 10:10).</li>
      </ul>

      <p>Communities around the world today keep the months this way, and the barley in Israel is reported each spring. <strong>The Father's calendar is being kept again, by people simply willing to look up.</strong></p>

      <h2>Why This Matters</h2>

      <p>If the heavens do not declare the month, then men do. And whoever declares the month controls the feasts. Whoever controls the feasts controls when Yahuah's people gather, when they fast, when they rejoice. <strong>That authority belongs to Yahuah alone, exercised through the witnesses He hung in the heavens.</strong></p>

      <div class="cf-pull">
        The renewed moon is not just one option among three. It is the turning Yahuah built into creation, the same instant for every man on earth, and it cannot be moved by anyone. To return to His calendar is to look up, to reckon by what the lights actually do, and to refuse any table that can be adjusted. The sky does not need a rabbi or a pope to interpret it. It speaks for itself, and the language it speaks is the language Yahuah taught it on the fourth day of the world.
      </div>

